  <blockquote class="evidence">X began cropping timeline images with a saliency algorithm in 2018. In 2021 it moved to showing standard aspect ratio photos in full on iOS and Android, launched that to everyone, and added a true preview of the image in the composer, according to its engineering blog.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: X engineering blog, "Sharing learnings about our image cropping algorithm," May 19, 2021, read October 14, 2026. https://blog.x.com/engineering/en_us/topics/insights/2021/sharing-learnings-about-our-image-cropping-algorithm</p>

  <p>The layout ratios that circulate for multi-picture posts (one set for two, another for three, a grid for four) come from size guides. They don't appear on X's help center, in its developer documentation or on its blog. They are observations, with no X page behind them.</p>

  <p>What works in production instead (this is our practice, not X's spec):</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Leave room on every side.</strong> Put the subject and any words in the middle of the frame, with margin all round, so a tighter cell can't reach them.</li>
    <li><strong>Load every picture before you judge.</strong> The composer preview is the only view X stands behind. Read it with all four pictures in place.</li>
    <li><strong>Reorder in the composer.</strong> On iOS, X's help says you can tap and hold a photo to drag it into a new place once two or more are selected.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How long can a video on X be?</h2>

  <p>For a standard account posting on the web: 140 seconds and 512MB. Past that, the answer depends on how you upload and which X page you believe.</p>

